//RS /// caller still holds an index into the entry module's items and removes the
//RS /// entry itself once the build has run (`Driver/mod.rs`).
//RS ///
//RS /// So the invariant is not "sema always strips". It is "the one check that keeps
//RS /// its build entry has exactly one caller, and that caller strips
//RS /// unconditionally." Written as a comment, that pairing is review discipline and
//RS /// rots the moment a second caller appears — and the failure mode is not a
//RS /// diagnostic but an `emit_func` internal compiler error on a program sema just
//RS /// accepted, which is what this card is. Check it instead.
